    "answer": {
      "short": "Squaring introduces an extraneous solution: $\\sqrt{x}$ cannot equal $-1$. At $x=1$, the denominator is $2$, so the function is continuous there.",
      "worked": "For real $x$ in the domain, $x\\ge0$ and $\\sqrt{x}\\ge0$. Thus $\\sqrt{x}=-1$ has no solution. Squaring produces the candidate $x=1$, but it fails the original equation:\n$$\\begin{align*}1+\\sqrt{1}&=2\\ne0,\\\\f(1)&=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe denominator is positive throughout the domain, and $f$ is continuous at $x=1$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
